from tnsoc import *
S(9,11,"Asha felt a small tremor in class. \"Why does the ground shake?\" she asked.",
 ["The Earth has layers: the crust, mantle and core. The crust is broken into large plates that move slowly. This is explained by plate tectonics, and the movement causes endogenetic (internal) processes.",
  "Plate movements cause earthquakes, volcanoes and the folding and faulting of rocks. Earthquakes start at the focus and the epicentre is the point above it on the surface. Volcanoes throw out lava, ash and gases.",
  "Fold mountains such as the Himalayas form where plates collide, and the Pacific Ring of Fire has many volcanoes and earthquakes."],
 ["crust, mantle, core","plate tectonics","earthquake","epicentre","volcano","fold mountain"],
 ("How are fold mountains formed?",["Two plates collide.","Rocks are pushed up and folded.","The Himalayas formed this way."]),
 ("The epicentre is deep underground.",["The focus is underground.","The epicentre is on the surface."]),
 ["Earth has crust, mantle, core.","Plates move.","Earthquake: focus and epicentre.","Himalayas are fold mountains."],
 ["m|The outer layer of the Earth is the|crust|mantle|core|Thin and solid.","t|Volcanoes throw out lava.|T|Yes.","m|The point above the focus is the|epicentre|crater|vent|On the surface.","t|Plates never move.|F|They move slowly.","m|The Himalayas are|fold mountains|plateaus|deltas|Plate collision.","s|What is the Ring of Fire in?|pacific|Pacific Ocean."])
S(9,12,"Meena saw a river carve a valley in a picture. \"Rivers cut rock!\" she said.",
 ["Exogenetic (external) processes work from outside on the surface. Weathering breaks rocks in place by temperature changes, water and plants. Erosion carries material away by running water, wind, glaciers and waves, and deposition leaves it elsewhere.",
  "Rivers form V-shaped valleys, waterfalls, meanders, flood plains and deltas. Glaciers carve U-shaped valleys, wind makes dunes in deserts and waves make cliffs, caves and beaches.",
  "These processes slowly shape the landscape and create fertile land."],
 ["weathering","erosion","deposition","meander","delta","glacier"],
 ("How is a delta formed?",["A river slows near the sea.","It drops its sediment.","The land built up is a delta."]),
 ("Weathering and erosion are the same.",["Weathering breaks rock in place.","Erosion moves it."]),
 ["Weathering breaks rock.","Erosion carries it.","Deposition lays it down.","Rivers, glaciers, wind, waves shape land."],
 ["m|Deposition means|laying down of material|breaking rock|melting|Final stage.","t|Glaciers carve U-shaped valleys.|T|Yes.","m|A landform at the river mouth is a|delta|dune|crater|Built of silt.","t|Wind makes dunes.|T|Yes.","m|Breaking of rock in place is|weathering|erosion|deposition|By climate.","s|What do waves create on coasts?|cliffs|And beaches."])
S(9,13,"Raju checked the weather before leaving. \"What is air made of?\" he asked.",
 ["The atmosphere is the layer of gases around the Earth. It is made of nitrogen (78 percent), oxygen (21 percent) and small amounts of other gases. Its layers are the troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere and exosphere. Weather happens in the troposphere.",
  "Weather elements are temperature, pressure, winds, humidity and rainfall. Climate is the average weather over many years. Winds blow from high to low pressure, and rain falls as relief, convectional or cyclonic rainfall.",
  "The ozone in the stratosphere shields us from ultraviolet rays. Pollution and greenhouse gases cause global warming."],
 ["atmosphere","troposphere","weather","climate","ozone","humidity"],
 ("Why is the ozone layer important?",["It absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays.","It protects living things.","It is in the stratosphere."]),
 ("Weather and climate mean the same.",["Weather is for a short time.","Climate is the long-term average."]),
 ["Air is mostly nitrogen and oxygen.","Troposphere holds weather.","Ozone blocks UV.","Climate is long-term."],
 ["m|The gas in greatest amount is|nitrogen|oxygen|carbon dioxide|78 percent.","t|Weather occurs in the troposphere.|T|Yes.","m|The ozone layer is in the|stratosphere|troposphere|exosphere|Protects from UV.","t|Winds blow from low to high pressure.|F|High to low.","m|Average weather for years is|climate|weather|season|Long term.","s|Name one weather element.|temperature|Or rainfall."])
S(9,14,"Asha poured water into a glass. \"Where does all our water come from?\" she asked.",
 ["The hydrosphere is all the water on Earth, in oceans, rivers, lakes, ice and underground. About 71 percent of the surface is covered by water, but most is salty. The water cycle moves water by evaporation, condensation and precipitation.",
  "Ocean water moves as waves, tides and currents. Tides rise and fall because of the Moon's and the Sun's pull, and spring tides occur at full and new moon. Warm and cold currents change climate and fishing.",
  "The ocean floor has continental shelves, slopes, deep plains and trenches. Fresh water must be saved, as only a small part is usable."],
 ["hydrosphere","water cycle","tide","ocean current","continental shelf","trench"],
 ("Why do tides occur?",["The Moon and the Sun pull the water.","Water rises and falls.","Spring tides come at full and new moon."]),
 ("Most of Earth's water is fresh.",["Most is salty ocean water.","Fresh water is small."]),
 ["71 percent of Earth is water.","Water cycle.","Tides by Moon's pull.","Currents affect climate."],
 ["m|Water vapour turning to liquid is|condensation|evaporation|precipitation|Cooling.","t|Oceans cover most of the Earth.|T|Yes.","m|Tides are caused by the|Moon and Sun|wind|earthquake|Gravity.","t|Ocean currents never affect climate.|F|They do.","m|The deepest part of the ocean floor is a|trench|shelf|plain|Very deep.","s|Which process makes water vapour?|evaporation|Heat."])
S(9,15,"Meena saw a forest, a desert and a pond in a book. \"All life, but so different,\" she said.",
 ["The biosphere is the zone of life, where land, water and air meet. It includes plants, animals and microbes. Ecosystems are communities of living things and their surroundings, linked by food chains and food webs.",
  "Major biomes include forests, grasslands, deserts and tundra, each with its own climate and life. Forests give wood and oxygen, grasslands support grazing and deserts have specially adapted plants.",
  "Biodiversity, the variety of life, is under threat from forest loss and pollution. Protected areas such as national parks and biosphere reserves, for example the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve, help conserve it."],
 ["biosphere","ecosystem","food chain","biome","biodiversity","biosphere reserve"],
 ("Why save biodiversity?",["Every species has a role.","It gives food and medicine.","Losses cannot be undone."]),
 ("A food chain has no plants.",["Plants are the producers.","Food chains begin with them."]),
 ["Biosphere is the zone of life.","Ecosystem links living and non-living.","Biomes: forest, desert, grassland.","Reserves protect biodiversity."],
 ["m|The zone of life is the|biosphere|lithosphere|atmosphere only|Where life exists.","t|Plants are producers.|T|Yes.","m|A community plus its surroundings is an|ecosystem|biome only|desert|Linked by energy.","t|Deserts have no life.|F|Adapted life exists.","m|The Nilgiri is a|biosphere reserve|desert|delta|Conservation area.","s|The variety of life is called?|biodiversity|Bio plus diversity."])
S(9,16,"Raju noticed the new road cut through green fields. \"Does progress cost nature?\" he asked.",
 ["Man and environment are closely linked. Early humans depended on nature, but with tools and technology people now change the environment through farming, industry and cities. This brings both benefit and harm.",
  "Human activities cause deforestation, soil erosion, air and water pollution and climate change. Different regions show different lives, such as the Eskimos in cold lands and the Bedouins in deserts, which show how people adapt to their surroundings.",
  "Sustainable development means meeting present needs without harming the future. We can save the environment by planting trees, saving water and energy, and reducing waste."],
 ["environment","deforestation","pollution","adaptation","sustainable development","conservation"],
 ("What is sustainable development?",["Meeting today's needs.","Without harming future generations.","By using resources wisely."]),
 ("Humans do not affect the environment.",["Human activity changes it greatly.","Farming and industry cause change."]),
 ["People and nature are linked.","Humans cause pollution and deforestation.","People adapt to their regions.","Sustainable development protects the future."],
 ["m|Cutting forests is|deforestation|afforestation|irrigation|Loss of trees.","t|Pollution harms health.|T|Yes.","m|Sustainable development protects|future generations|only profit|only cities|Wise use.","t|Eskimos live in deserts.|F|They live in cold lands.","m|Planting trees is|afforestation|erosion|pollution|Helps nature.","s|Name one way to save the environment.|plant trees|Or save water."])
S(9,17,"Asha used a map to find her way. \"What do these symbols mean?\" she asked.",
 ["A map is a drawing of the Earth's surface. Its parts include the title, scale, direction, legend and symbols. The scale shows how distances on the map relate to the ground, and the four main directions are north, south, east and west.",
  "Latitudes run east-west and longitudes run north-south, and together they give the location of a place as a grid. The Equator is 0 degrees latitude and the Prime Meridian is 0 degrees longitude. Time zones follow longitude.",
  "Maps show relief with contour lines, and we use colours and symbols for features. Topographic maps and the Survey of India sheets help us study areas. GIS and satellite images are modern aids."],
 ["map","scale","legend","latitude","longitude","contour"],
 ("How do latitude and longitude find a place?",["Latitude gives the north-south position.","Longitude gives the east-west position.","Together they give a point."]),
 ("The Equator is 0 degrees longitude.",["It is 0 degrees latitude.","The Prime Meridian is 0 degrees longitude."]),
 ["A map has scale, direction, legend.","Latitudes run east-west.","Longitudes run north-south.","Contours show relief."],
 ["m|The Equator is|0 degrees latitude|0 degrees longitude|90 degrees|Midway between poles.","t|A legend explains map symbols.|T|Yes.","m|Lines joining points of equal height are|contours|meridians|parallels|Relief.","t|Longitudes run east-west.|F|They run north-south.","m|The scale shows|distance relation|temperature|rainfall|Map to ground.","s|What is the 0-degree longitude called?|prime meridian|Through Greenwich."])
S(9,18,"Meena saw news of a flood on TV. \"How can people stay safe?\" she asked.",
 ["A disaster is a sudden event that causes great loss. Natural disasters include floods, cyclones, earthquakes, droughts and tsunamis, while man-made ones include fires and industrial accidents. Hazards become disasters when people are not prepared.",
  "Disaster management has stages: mitigation (reducing risk), preparedness, response and recovery. Early warnings, evacuation plans, shelters and first aid save lives. In India the National Disaster Management Authority leads the effort.",
  "Citizens should know safe places, keep an emergency kit and follow instructions. For earthquakes, drop, cover and hold; for floods move to higher ground. The 2004 tsunami in Tamil Nadu taught valuable lessons."],
 ["disaster","mitigation","preparedness","tsunami","NDMA","first aid"],
 ("What should you do in an earthquake?",["Drop to the floor.","Take cover under a sturdy table.","Hold on until shaking stops."]),
 ("Disasters cannot be reduced by preparation.",["Preparation reduces loss.","Early warning saves lives."]),
 ["Disasters cause sudden loss.","Stages: mitigation, preparedness, response, recovery.","NDMA leads in India.","Know safe places."],
 ["m|Reducing risk before a disaster is|mitigation|response|recovery|First stage.","t|Early warning saves lives.|T|Yes.","m|A tsunami hit Tamil Nadu in|2004|1990|2015|Boxing Day.","t|Floods are man-made disasters only.|F|They are mostly natural.","m|NDMA stands for National Disaster Management|Authority|Army|Agency|Leads policy.","s|During an earthquake, drop, cover and what?|hold|Hold on."])
